<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Annapolis, Maryland</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Annapolis is the capital of the US state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a>, on the Severn River near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chesapeake-bay">Chesapeake Bay</a>. Settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puritans">Puritans</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> as Providence in 1649, it became the colonial capital in 1694 and was renamed after Princess Anne. The Congress of the Confederation met there in 1783–1784, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> resigned his command, and it has been home to the US Naval Academy since 1845.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/annapolis-maryland.jpg" alt="Annapolis, Maryland" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Flickr high limitzz highlimitstudio (Flickr) · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Some_Annapolis_commercial_strip.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Annapolis lies on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chesapeake-bay">Chesapeake Bay</a> at the mouth of the Severn River, 25 miles south of Baltimore and about 30 miles east of Washington, D.C. The city is part of the Atlantic Coastal Plain and is relatively flat, its highest point only 50 feet above sea level. It is the closest state capital to the national capital and, in land area, the smallest of the capital cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. It has a strong maritime culture and is known as America's Sailing Capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1649 Puritan exiles from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, led by the third proprietary governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a>, William Stone, founded a settlement called Providence on the north shore of the Severn. The settlers later moved to a better-protected harbour on the south shore. The settlement there was known from 1683 as the Town at Proctor's, then as the Town at the Severn, and in 1694 it received the name Anne Arundel's Towne. In 1696 St. John's College was founded in the city.</p><p>In 1783–1784 Annapolis was the seat of the Confederation Congress, the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-continental-congress">Second Continental Congress</a>, and the temporary national capital. On 23 December 1783 General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> resigned his commission as commander of the Continental Army before the Congress in the new Maryland State House. The Congress ratified the Treaty of Paris, which ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>, there on 14 January 1784, so that Annapolis became the first peacetime capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. It was also the site of the 1786 Annapolis Convention, which called on the states to send delegates to the Constitutional Convention held at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> the next year.</p><p>The Maryland State House, begun in 1772, with the legislature first meeting there in 1779, is the oldest in continuous legislative use in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. It is topped by the largest wooden dome built without nails in the country. In 1861 the midshipmen of the Naval Academy, established in 1845, were moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newport-rhode-island">Newport, Rhode Island</a>, until 1865, and Camp Parole, a camp for paroled soldiers, was set up on the grounds of St. John's College; the Annapolis National Cemetery holds those who died. In July 1940 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlotte-grand-duchess-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchess Charlotte</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> arrived in Annapolis in exile.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Annapolis is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> and the county seat of Anne Arundel County, with a population of 40,812 at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. The state legislature, the governor's office and the appellate courts are located there. The United States Naval Academy stands adjacent to the city limits, and the city hosts each year the largest in-water sailboat show in the world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Annapolis, Maryland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Naval School, renamed the United States Naval Academy in 1850, opened at Fort Severn in <b>Annapolis, Maryland</b>, with 50 midshipmen.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-10">10 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Protestant revolt of 1689 the Crown took over, the Church of England was established, and the capital moved to <b>Annapolis</b> in 1694.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-maryland">Province of Maryland</a></li></ul></section>
